@page "/help"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false))

@using BgQuiz_Blazor.Client.Quiz
@using XgFilter_Razor
@using XgFilter_Razor.Components

@inject QuizController Controller
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>BgQuiz — Help</PageTitle>

@* Three grid children, one document (SPEC-help.md §5). The reading width that
   used to be an inline max-width on the container now sits on .help-intro and
   .help-doc, unchanged at 800px; the container itself has to be free to be
   wider, because at Bootstrap lg and up the contents block sits BESIDE the
   document as a sticky rail. Below lg the three fall back into ordinary block
   flow, which is exactly the spec's narrow case: the contents block renders
   once, under the <h1> and its lead, and no rail exists. One <nav> element
   either way — a second, CSS-hidden copy would put two navigations named
   "Contents" in the accessibility tree and duplicate every entry in the
   document, so the placement is CSS's job and the markup states it once. *@
<div class="container py-3 help-page">
    <header class="help-intro">
        <h1>How BgQuiz works</h1>

        <p class="lead">
            BgQuiz turns your own eXtreme Gammon analysis into practice: it shows you a
            position from one of your files, you answer it, and it scores your answer
            against XG's evaluation of that position.
        </p>
    </header>

    @* The contents block (SPEC-help.md §2): two levels, parts and their
       sections, every entry an in-page link. Rendered from HelpSections — the
       same table the headings below render from — so it cannot list a section
       the document does not have, or miss one it does.

       FilterHelp's own sections are deliberately NOT listed: they are the
       producer's structure, reachable through the Choose filters entry and
       their own fh-* anchors, and enumerating them here would be this repo
       restating another's outline.

       Nothing collapses and nothing is hidden — find-in-page sees the whole
       document — and there is no scroll-spy and no back-to-top by ruling.

       The hrefs go through AnchorHref for the reason the data section's pointer
       does: a bare "#fragment" resolves against <base href="/">, not against the
       current address, so on /help it would land the reader on Home. *@
    <nav class="help-contents" aria-labelledby="help-contents-label">
        <p id="help-contents-label" class="help-contents-label">Contents</p>
        <ul>
            @foreach (var part in HelpSections.Parts)
            {
                <li>
                    <a href="@AnchorHref(part.AnchorId)">@part.Heading</a>
                    <ul>
                        @foreach (var section in part.Sections)
                        {
                            <li><a href="@AnchorHref(section.AnchorId)">@section.Heading</a></li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="help-doc">

        @* The parts are the page's own structure (SPEC-help.md §1), so every part
           and section heading below renders its words and its id from HelpSections
           rather than spelling either here. Each part opens with at most one lead
           sentence saying what it covers — the only new prose this restructure
           adds, and deliberately nothing a section beneath it already says. *@
        <h2 class="h3 mt-5" id="@HelpSections.BeforeYouStart.AnchorId">@HelpSections.BeforeYouStart.Heading</h2>
        <p>
            What BgQuiz needs from you before it can quiz you, and what it does with
            the files you point it at.
        </p>

        @* Prerequisites, ahead of the flow. BgQuiz ships no sample files and does
           not work on every browser, so a reader can arrive with nothing it can
           act on — and everything below assumes those two things are already true.
           The browser sentence is rendered from FolderPickDisplay rather than
           restated, so this lead and Home's line beside the pick button say the
           identical thing; the file names come from the constants the stores
           actually write, the same discipline the caps and stats-file prose use. *@
        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.WhatYouNeed.AnchorId">@HelpSections.WhatYouNeed.Heading</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Your own analyzed positions.</strong> BgQuiz quizzes you on
                <code>.xg</code> match files and <code>.xgp</code> position files
                that you already have. It ships none of its own, so you need a folder
                of them on this machine before anything below applies.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A supported browser.</strong> @FolderPickDisplay.SupportedBrowsers
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Two small files, written into that folder.</strong> BgQuiz
                saves <code>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</code> (your lifetime record) and
                <code>@SavedFiltersDocument.FileName</code> (filters you save by name)
                beside your problem files. It writes nothing else, and never changes
                the files it quizzes you on.
            </li>
        </ul>

        @* Data ownership and storage, directly after the prerequisites and ahead of
           the flow: a reader who wants to know what happens to their positions wants
           that answer before handing over a folder, not twelve sections later. The
           "Nothing leaves your machine" bullet that used to close the list above was
           *moved* here rather than copied — the same page asserting it twice is
           exactly the duplication a consolidated section is prone to.

           The browser entries are rendered from the constants that write them
           (MixDraft.StorageKey, QuizSettings.StorageKey, QuizLiveMarker.StorageKey),
           never typed as prose
           literals, so a name a reader checks in devtools cannot drift from the name
           the code stores under — the discipline the file names and pick caps already
           use. The two files BgQuiz writes into the folder are deliberately NOT
           restated: they are named from their own constants in the list above, and
           this section points back at them.

           The filter panel's storage is a POINTER, not a description. XgFilter_Razor
           owns that prose and renders it from the panel's own constants inside
           FilterHelp's storage section, which this page already embeds under Choose
           filters. Both halves of the link below — where it lands and what it says —
           come from that section's exported identity (FilterHelp's
           StorageSectionAnchorId / StorageSectionHeading), never spelled here: a
           rename there is a compile error in this page rather than a link that
           still renders and now lies. Naming or describing the panel's keys here
           would be a second encoding of a producer's internals that no test in this
           repo could notice drifting — the same ruling that removed this page's
           facet prose. *@
        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.YourDataStaysYours.AnchorId">@HelpSections.YourDataStaysYours.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            Your positions and matches are yours, and BgQuiz treats them that way. It
            reads the files you point it at and parses them inside your browser; they
            are never uploaded, and neither are the positions in them, the answers you
            give, or your scores. There is no account to create, and the server BgQuiz
            is downloaded from has nothing to receive any of it — it hands your browser
            the app and plays no further part.
        </p>
        <p>
            BgQuiz does keep three small entries in the browser itself, so your setup is
            still there when you come back:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>@MixDraft.StorageKey</code> — your weighted mix as last shown
                on screen, in this site's local storage. It survives reloading the
                page and closing the browser, and it holds the mix settings only:
                the categories, their percentages and order, and the quiz length.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>@QuizSettings.StorageKey</code> — your choices from the
                <em>Settings</em> page, also in this site's local storage. It holds
                those settings and nothing else, and it too survives reloading and
                closing the browser.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>@QuizLiveMarker.StorageKey</code> — a single mark noting that a
                quiz was running, which is how BgQuiz can tell you afterwards that a
                reload reset it. It is kept in <em>session</em> storage, which belongs
                to this browser tab alone: another tab never sees it, and it
                disappears when you close this one.
            </li>
        </ul>
        @* What a reader can DO about the three entries above (issue #54). The
           sentence this replaced put the whole answer behind "your browser's
           developer tools" — a place most readers have never opened, and the only
           route it offered. The ownership point is worthless to someone who cannot
           act on it, so the route a general reader already has leads: the browser's
           own setting for clearing what a site has stored.

           Named by what it does, never by a menu path — every browser words and
           places it differently, the same class of claim as the permission-prompt
           text FolderPickDisplay rules out quoting. Hedged accordingly.

           The devtools route survives as a trailing parenthesis, signposted as
           optional. It is the only way to inspect the three entries individually,
           which is what makes the key names above findable at all; what it must not
           be again is the sentence's premise.

           "Nothing of yours goes with them" is the ownership point restated as a
           consequence, and it is the question a non-technical reader actually has
           when told to clear a site's data — will this take my positions? It is not
           a restatement of the paragraph below, which says where BgQuiz *writes*;
           this says what clearing the browser does not reach. *@
        <p>
            All three live in this browser, on this machine — not on any server — and
            they are yours to delete. Every browser has a setting for clearing what a
            site has stored, usually with the history and cookie controls, worded a
            little differently in each: using it on this site removes all three at
            once. Nothing of yours goes with them — the files in your problem folder
            are untouched — and BgQuiz simply starts again with its defaults. (If you
            use your browser's developer tools, the three are listed there under this
            site's storage.)
        </p>
        <p>
            The filter panel keeps its own settings in your browser as well —
            <a href="@PanelStorageHref">@FilterHelp.StorageSectionHeading</a>, in
            <em>Choose filters</em> below, lists exactly what those are.
        </p>
        <p>
            Everything else BgQuiz keeps, it writes into your own problem folder: the
            two files named above, and nothing besides.
        </p>
        @* How to leave (issue #51). The section that has just accounted for
           everything BgQuiz keeps is the one place this belongs: the answer to
           "can I just close it?" is a consequence of that account, not a new fact,
           and a reader who has been told what is stored where will otherwise
           assume a quiz in progress is among it.

           No button was added for this, deliberately — closing the tab IS the exit
           for an app with no account and nothing pending, and a "finish and quit"
           control would invent an obligation the app does not have.

           The write-access caveat is a POINTER, not a restatement: Lifetime stats
           below already draws that distinction in full, and a second telling here
           would be the copy that rots when the first one changes. The two files
           are likewise named only in Before you start — hence "your lifetime
           record", the words that section itself uses. *@
        <p>
            That is also why it is safe to close the tab whenever you like,
            mid-quiz included: nothing here is waiting to be saved. Your lifetime
            record is written to your folder as you answer rather than at the end,
            so the answers you have already given are already in it — and the
            things that do not outlive the tab, your folder pick and the quiz in
            progress, were never meant to. (In browsers that cannot write into your
            folder, nothing is being recorded to begin with — see
            <em>Lifetime stats</em> below.)
        </p>

        <h2 class="h3 mt-5" id="@HelpSections.SettingUpAQuiz.AnchorId">@HelpSections.SettingUpAQuiz.Heading</h2>
        <p>
            How you choose which of your positions a quiz draws from, and how to keep
            those choices for next time.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.PickYourFolder.AnchorId">@HelpSections.PickYourFolder.Heading</h3>
        @* The over-cap sentence carries the random draw because this is where the
           actionable half belongs: Home's truncation notice reports one pick and
           deliberately stops there, so "pick it again and you get different files"
           has nowhere else to live. Not a restatement of that notice — it says what
           happens NEXT time, which a status line about THIS pick cannot.

           Deliberately no promise of eventual full coverage: the draw is uniform
           per pick, not a queue, so "you will see all of it" would be a guarantee
           the pick does not make. The claim stops at "not shut out permanently". *@
        <p>
            On the home page, choose the local folder holding your <code>.xg</code> match
            files and <code>.xgp</code> position files. Only files directly in that folder
            are used — subfolders are ignored. One pick reads up to
            @PickedFileLimits.MaxXgFileCount <code>.xg</code> files and
            @PickedFileLimits.MaxXgpFileCount <code>.xgp</code> files, each up to
            @(PickedFileLimits.MaxFileMegabytes) MB. The two limits are separate because
            a match file holds a whole match's worth of decisions while a position file
            holds one, so the same number of them is nowhere near the same amount of
            work. If a folder holds more of either than that, BgQuiz quizzes you on the
            ones it read and tells you on the home page how many it left — nothing fails,
            and the quiz simply does not include them. Which ones it reads are drawn at
            random each time, so picking that folder again reads a different selection:
            nothing in an oversized folder is shut out permanently, it just won't all
            arrive in one pick.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.ChooseFilters.AnchorId">@HelpSections.ChooseFilters.Heading</h3>
        @* App framing only, per umbrella #36: FilterHelp now documents the panel's
           chrome as well as its facets — the disclosure and its hidden-active
           badge, Apply as the only commit and both of its disabled states, Clear
           filters — so the sentences that used to describe those controls here are
           gone. They were the same defect the facet list was, one tier up: a second
           encoding of the producer's behavior that no test in this repo could
           notice drifting. The producer's Pitfalls now draw the line explicitly —
           a host says where the panel sits in this app and what applying it unlocks
           here, never what its controls do.

           What is left is exactly the half FilterHelp cannot know: that in BgQuiz
           an applied filter is the gate on Start (with the rider that navigating
           the panel is not an edit, so looking around never costs the reader that
           gate — phrased without naming a control, since the disclosure has no
           antecedent this early and belongs to FilterHelp to introduce), and that
           Shuffle order is this app's control, not the panel's. Extending any of it
           means extending FilterHelp in XgFilter_Razor, never restoring prose
           here. *@
        <p>
            On the home page, the filter panel narrows which decisions the quiz will
            ask you.
        </p>
        <p>
            Filters must be <strong>applied</strong> before Start becomes available —
            editing any control un-applies them until you press <em>Apply Filter</em>
            again. Only edits count: expanding or collapsing part of the panel to see
            what is in it never un-applies anything. Optionally tick
            <em>Shuffle order</em> to see positions in a random order rather than in
            file order.
        </p>
        @* The count line, previously undocumented anywhere. Three things about it are
           easy to read wrongly — it counts matching *decisions* rather than problems
           you will be shown, it counts each position once, and it describes the
           filters alone — and the last is load-bearing once a mix is applied, since
           the quiz can then be far smaller than the number on screen. The dedupe
           sentence is issue #104's: the count has always been deduplicated, and the
           gap between it and a file count reads as a bug while nothing names it.
           Home states each caveat beside the number in its own voice; this stays
           prose, per FolderPickDisplay's "Help's prose stays prose" rule (a shared
           constant is earned only by two surfaces rendering the same sentence). *@
        <p>
            Each time you press Apply, a line below the panel says how many decisions
            your filters matched. That is the pool your quiz is drawn from — and it
            counts <em>decisions</em>, not problems you will be shown: a position whose
            dice leave no legal play is counted here and then skipped for you at quiz
            time. Repeated positions are counted once: where the same position turns
            up more than once in your collection, the extra copies are left out of
            that number, and the line says how many they were. It also describes the
            filters only. So if you have applied a weighted mix, the mix draws the
            quiz from that pool rather than presenting all of it, and the quiz
            can be much smaller than the number shown. Starting is what composes
            the mix, and the quiz page then tells you exactly what it drew.
        </p>
        @* The answer-type breakdown that rides under the count. It answers a
           question the count cannot — what the collection is made of, rather than
           how big it is — so it earns its own paragraph instead of a clause, and
           that paragraph leads with the zero reading, which is the one a reader
           would otherwise mistake for a rendering fault.

           The five bucket names are deliberately NOT recited here. They are copy
           owned by AnswerTypeDisplay and rendered on Home; a list of them in this
           prose would be a second encoding that drifts silently the first time a
           label is reworded — the same reasoning that removed this page's facet
           list. What this paragraph carries is what the labels cannot say for
           themselves: that the list is exhaustive, and what a zero means. *@
        <p>
            Under that number, BgQuiz breaks the pool down by the kind of answer its
            decisions call for — checker plays, and each of the five cube verdicts.
            Every kind is listed every time, so a kind showing
            <strong>0</strong> is a result rather than an omission: it says your
            folder holds none of that kind under the filters you applied. If you
            suspect your saved positions lean one way, this is where it shows.
        </p>

        @* Panel reference, rendered by XgFilter_Razor's own FilterHelp rather than
           restated here. Every facet — and, since umbrella #36, the chrome that
           governs them all — is the panel's behavior, and the panel is the
           producer's; a description written on this page would be a second encoding
           of lib rules that drifts the moment those rules change, with no test
           anywhere able to notice. The depth facet's redesign into per-mode clauses
           is what settled the facet half: BgQuiz was spared only because it had
           never described that facet, which is luck, not a discipline. The
           producer's Pitfalls make it a rule — a host embeds FilterHelp and adds
           app-level framing only, and prose FilterHelp lacks is extended there,
           never written here.

           Placed last in the section so the app's own framing — that Apply gates
           Start, what the count means — reads as continuous prose before the
           reference block.

           HeadingLevel is read off this page's own outline rather than chosen: the
           parts are <h2> and the sections beneath them <h3> (the "h4" class on a
           section is Bootstrap sizing, not a level), and this block is embedded
           inside one of those sections, so its lead belongs at h4 and its own
           sections land at h5. It was 3 while the page was flat; the extra tier the
           parts added (SPEC-help.md §4) pushed it down one, which stays inside
           FilterHelp's 1..5 range and so needs nothing from the producer. The
           hard-coded h4/h5 pair this replaced had been jumping h2 → h4 here the
           whole time — invisible on screen, visible in a screen reader's outline —
           which is why the producer made the level required rather than defaulted.
           Nothing the block renders is an h2 or an h3, so the pinned part and
           section skeletons are untouched either way. It is render-only otherwise:
           no state, no JS, nothing else for this page to wire. *@
        <FilterHelp HeadingLevel="4" />

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.SaveFilters.AnchorId">@HelpSections.SaveFilters.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            Above the filter panel, <em>Saved Filters</em> keeps named copies of
            filter settings you want back later — a race-position set, one opponent,
            a particular band of mistake sizes. Type a name and press <em>Save</em>
            to store the filters as they currently stand; <em>Load</em> puts a saved
            set back into the panel below, and <em>Delete</em> removes one (both
            overwriting and deleting ask you to confirm first). A loaded set arrives
            as an edit, not as a decision — Start stays disabled until you press
            Apply, so you are free to adjust it first.
        </p>
        @* Both file names are rendered from SavedFiltersDocument's constants — the
           producer's SSOT for the document identity and its two-name migration rule
           (read canonical first, fall back to the legacy name only when the
           canonical file is absent, write only canonical, never delete the legacy
           file). The legacy sentence states the still-read fallback out loud: an
           existing tester will see that file beside the newly-documented canonical
           one, and a silently-read file would cut against this section's
           storage-transparency posture. The rule is standing, so the sentence
           doesn't rot. *@
        <p>
            Saved filters live in a file named <code>@SavedFiltersDocument.FileName</code>
            inside your problem folder, so they belong to that folder and travel with
            it. If a folder has filters saved under the earlier name
            <code>@SavedFiltersDocument.LegacyFileName</code>, BgQuiz still reads
            them from there; the next save writes <code>@SavedFiltersDocument.FileName</code>,
            and the older file is left untouched. They need the same write access
            lifetime stats need: without it you can still load filters saved
            earlier, but not save, change, or delete them. If the file can't be
            read, BgQuiz leaves it exactly as it found it and switches saving off
            rather than risk overwriting it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.WeightedMix.AnchorId">@HelpSections.WeightedMix.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            Below the filters, <em>Weighted mix</em> composes the quiz out of your
            <em>Lifetime stats</em> (below) instead of simply taking everything the
            filters matched. Add categories — never seen, ever got wrong, not seen in
            so many days, average equity loss over a threshold, wrong more than some
            share of the time — give each a percentage, and make the percentages
            total 100. You get a quiz built to those proportions from the problems
            your filters selected.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Row order settles overlap.</strong> A problem that fits two
                of your categories counts toward the <em>earlier</em> row, so the
                ↑ / ↓ buttons are how you say which category gets first claim.
                <em>Everything else</em> catches whatever the rows above it didn't.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Quiz length is optional.</strong> Left blank, the mix takes
                every problem its categories can reach; set it and the percentages
                divide up that many problems instead.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A mix that applies owns the order.</strong> Its own
                <em>Random order</em> setting then decides how problems are
                presented, and the page's Shuffle order checkbox is disabled — your
                choice there is kept, and comes back when you turn the mix off.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No lifetime stats, no mix.</strong> The mix is what your
                stats record composes into a quiz, so it appears only for a folder
                that can save stats <em>and</em> already has some — on a folder
                you've never quizzed from, there is nothing yet to weight by, and
                the section simply isn't there. Run one quiz from it and the mix is
                offered from then on. If the stats turn out to be unusable when you
                press Start, BgQuiz refuses rather than quietly running an
                unweighted quiz: it offers to start that one quiz without the mix,
                and keeps the mix you built either way.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Turn it on with <em>Mix applies</em>.</strong> The mix you
                compose does nothing until you check that box (after applying the
                filters — the mix draws from the filtered pool), and what runs is
                always exactly the mix shown on screen. Unchecking turns it off
                without losing anything. Your rows keep — across pages, folder
                changes, and reloads — but the checkbox itself resets when you
                change folders or reload, so check it again to weight the next
                quiz. If the mix on screen is broken while the box is checked,
                Start waits until you fix it or uncheck.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="h3 mt-5" id="@HelpSections.Answering.AnchorId">@HelpSections.Answering.Heading</h2>
        <p>
            What each problem asks of you, and how the answer you give is scored.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.AnswerThePosition.AnchorId">@HelpSections.AnswerThePosition.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            Each problem is one of two kinds, and the board tells you which:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Checker play.</strong> The dice are rolled, and you enter the play you
                would make by clicking the board — see <em>Making a checker play</em> below.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Cube decision.</strong> The board is read-only. Answer with the two
                rows of radio buttons beside the Submit button: what the player on roll
                should do (no double, double, or too good to double), and how the opponent
                should respond if doubled (take or pass). Submit lights up once both are
                chosen. Every option is offered for every position — <em>Too good</em> is
                never hidden, and <em>No double</em> with <em>Pass</em> can be chosen even
                though it is never right (if they'd pass, cashing beats playing on; the
                solution says so).
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Press <em>Submit</em> to answer, or <em>Skip</em> to move on without answering.
        </p>
        @* The problem's locator (SPEC-quiz-view.md §4, issue
           halheinrich/backgammon#115), homed here per SPEC-help.md §7's queued-copy
           ruling: it belongs to the part that owns the behaviour, and this is the
           surface the reader is looking at while answering — the one composition
           where the chip is the only thing naming the file at all.

           One sentence, and it names no chrome: the label's own wording lives in
           ProblemLocator's constants, and prose quoting them would be a second copy
           to keep in step. "When the file holds a whole match" is how the .xgp
           branch is covered without the page having to explain file formats. *@
        <p>
            Under the board, a small label names the file each position came from and, when
            the file holds a whole match, its game and move number — enough to find the
            position again in eXtreme Gammon.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.MakingACheckerPlay.AnchorId">@HelpSections.MakingACheckerPlay.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            Plays are entered with single clicks — there is nothing to drag, and no
            pick-up-and-put-down. The board updates after every click, and only clicks that keep
            a legal full play reachable are accepted — anything else simply does nothing, so you
            cannot build an illegal play, and you cannot leave part of the roll unplayed when it
            could have been played. Landing on a lone opposing checker hits it automatically.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Clicking on a point you occupy moves a checker from there:</strong> it
                advances by one die straight away. When either die could play from that point, the
                <em>leftmost</em> die shown on the board is used — click the dice to swap them
                (see below). When the move carries the checker off the board, it bears off; that
                is just an ordinary move.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Clicking on a point that's empty or contains an opposing blot brings
                checkers there.</strong> If the point can be made, it is: two of your checkers land
                on it in the fewest moves, hitting anything in the way. With ordinary dice that
                uses both and finishes the play; with doubles you may still have dice left. If the
                point cannot be made, one checker that can reach it goes there instead. When there
                are two equally short ways to do it, the click does nothing; make the moves one at
                a time instead. This works even while you have a checker on the bar — see
                <em>Clicking on the bar</em>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Clicking on the bar enters a checker</strong>, and always works while you
                have one there. When both dice could enter, the <em>leftmost</em> die is used —
                swap the dice to enter on the other point, or click the entry point directly. While
                any checker of yours is on the bar it must enter before anything else can move —
                but you can still click any point that is empty or holds an opposing blot: the
                entry happens first, and the rest of the roll brings a checker to the point you
                clicked. That checker need not be the one that entered — with a 2-1 from the bar,
                clicking an opposing blot in your home board enters with the two and hits with the
                one, as long as there is exactly one way to do it. Clicking a point you occupy does
                nothing while you are on the bar, even one the entering checker could reach: enter
                first, then move normally.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Clicking on the checker tray bears off as many checkers as the roll
                allows</strong>, whenever there is a unique way to do it. If two ways bear off
                equally many, the tray does nothing — bear off point by point instead, by clicking
                each checker's own point.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Click the dice to swap them, or to submit.</strong> While the play is
                unfinished, clicking the dice swaps the order they are shown in — which changes
                which one a checker click uses — without disturbing the moves you have already
                made. (Doubles have nothing to swap.) Once the play is finished, clicking the dice
                submits it, exactly as the <em>Submit</em> button does.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Change your mind — <em>Undo last</em> and <em>Undo all</em>.</strong> Undo
                last takes back the most recent move; Undo all restores the starting position.
                <em>Submit</em> stays disabled until the whole roll has been played.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.Scoring.AnchorId">@HelpSections.Scoring.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            A submitted answer is scored against XG's analysis of that position. Correct means
            you chose a play or action with no equity loss; otherwise the equity you gave up is
            recorded. Skipped problems are counted separately and never affect your accuracy.
            Scores are kept in four buckets: checker plays, doubling decisions, take decisions,
            and the total across all three.
        </p>

        <h2 class="h3 mt-5" id="@HelpSections.AfterTheQuiz.AnchorId">@HelpSections.AfterTheQuiz.Heading</h2>
        <p>
            What you can look at once a problem is answered, and once a run is over.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.ReviewTheSolution.AnchorId">@HelpSections.ReviewTheSolution.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            After you submit, the board switches to the solution view: XG's full analysis panel,
            with the recorded play marked <strong>*</strong> and your answer marked
            <strong>†</strong> when it differs. <em>Continue</em> moves to the next problem;
            <em>Redo</em> re-opens the same position so you can try it again for practice.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Your first answer is the one that counts.</strong> Redo does not take it
            back — a retry is scored and shown to you exactly like the first attempt, so you
            can see how it would have gone, but it changes nothing: not your score, not your
            lifetime record. Retry as often as you like. A skip works the same way: once you
            have skipped a problem, redoing it does not un-skip it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.StatsAndFinishing.AnchorId">@HelpSections.StatsAndFinishing.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            <em>Show stats</em> opens a running scoreboard at any point mid-quiz, and returns you
            to exactly where you were. When the last problem is answered you land on the summary
            page, where you can restart with the same filters or start over with new ones.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.LifetimeStats.AnchorId">@HelpSections.LifetimeStats.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            In browsers that can grant write access to a folder (Chrome and Edge), BgQuiz
            also keeps a <em>lifetime</em> record: how often each position has been quizzed,
            how often you got it right, and when you last saw it. It lives in a small file
            named <code>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</code> inside your problem folder, right
            beside the files it tracks, and is updated after each problem you answer.
            Like your problem files, it never leaves your machine — it is written directly
            to your own folder, and nothing is uploaded anywhere.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Only answered problems count, and only your first answer.</strong>
                Skips, plays XG never listed, and positions skipped for you are not part of
                your lifetime record. Neither are practice retries after a <em>Redo</em>: a
                problem records the answer you gave it first, once.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A cube position records two decisions.</strong> The double and the
                take are counted separately, the same way the in-quiz score counts them — so
                getting one half right and the other wrong reads as one correct out of two,
                not as a single wrong answer.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Other browsers still quiz.</strong> Where the browser can't save into
                the folder (Firefox, Safari), or write access isn't granted, the quiz itself is
                unchanged — the same problems, the same filters, the same scoreboard. What is
                lost is the record that builds up <em>across</em> sessions of which problems
                give you difficulty: nothing is written, so nothing accumulates. The home page
                tells you which applies when you pick.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>An unreadable stats file is left alone.</strong> If the file can't be
                read (edited by hand, or written by a newer BgQuiz), the quiz runs without
                recording and the file is not touched.
            </li>
            @* Names both files from the app's own naming, like every other file name
               on this page — the prose cannot drift from what is written. The
               set-aside name depends on which version the old file was, which this
               page has no way of knowing, so it states the rule and shows the rule
               applied rather than promising one particular name. Says what happens
               and what it costs; the reason it can't be carried forward is a fact
               about the file, not an invitation to explain position identity to
               someone who came here to quiz. *@
            <li>
                <strong>Stats from an earlier version start over.</strong> BgQuiz changed
                how it recognises a position, so a stats file written by an earlier version
                can't be carried forward. The first quiz you run in that folder sets the old
                file aside — nothing is deleted — under a name that records the version it
                came from, such as <code>@QuizStatsFile.RetiredNameFor(1)</code>, and starts
                a fresh <code>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</code> beside it. The quiz tells you
                the name it used.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="h3 mt-5" id="@HelpSections.Reference.AnchorId">@HelpSections.Reference.Heading</h2>
        <p>
            The details that belong to no single step, and where to send what you find.
        </p>

        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.ThingsWorthKnowing.AnchorId">@HelpSections.ThingsWorthKnowing.Heading</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Positions with nothing to decide are skipped for you.</strong> When the
                dice leave the player on roll nothing to do — or only one legal play, so the
                move is forced — the position is passed over silently: you never see it, and it
                does not count as a skip.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A play XG never listed counts as a skip, not a wrong answer.</strong> If
                you submit a legal play that isn't among the analyzed candidates, there is no
                equity loss to record against it, so it scores as a skip. You still see the
                solution.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A cube position is two decisions, not one.</strong> The double and the
                take are scored independently, so one cube problem contributes one doubling
                decision and one take decision to your totals.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The doubling half is judged on your claim.</strong> <em>No double</em>
                and <em>Too good</em> are the same move at the board — you play on either
                way — but they are different claims about the position, and the quiz scores
                the claim. Saying <em>No double</em> where the position is too good to double
                is wrong with no equity lost; the solution names it as the right action with
                the wrong reason.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Clicking the dice advances.</strong> On the solution view, clicking the
                dice on the board does exactly what <em>Continue</em> does.
            </li>
            @* Deliberately about the control and its behaviour, never an inventory of
               what the panel holds — that list grows, and prose naming its entries
               would be wrong the day one is added. *@
            <li>
                <strong>The side panel folds out of the way.</strong> On a wide screen, the
                narrow strip down the left edge folds the blue panel away and hands its width
                back to the page — worth a click if the quiz feels crowded. Clicking the strip
                again brings the panel back, and the arrow on the strip always points the way
                the panel will move. The fold stays put while you work through the quiz —
                answering, skipping and continuing all leave it alone — and the panel comes
                back when you move to another page or reload. If you would rather it did
                not come back, turn on <em>Keep the navigation panel folded</em> on the
                <em>Settings</em> page.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Reloading the browser starts you over.</strong> Moving between pages
                inside the app is always safe — your folder, filters, and progress are kept. But a
                full browser reload (F5) discards all of it, and you will need to pick your folder
                again. Your lifetime stats file is not lost — it lives in your folder, and picking
                the folder again resumes it.
            </li>
        </ul>

        @* Feedback. Same href as Home's footer link, from the one AppInfo value, so
           the version in the subject can never be the version of some other build.
           Naming the version in the prose too is deliberate: a tester who mails from
           a different device still knows what to quote. *@
        <h3 class="h4 mt-4" id="@HelpSections.SendFeedback.AnchorId">@HelpSections.SendFeedback.Heading</h3>
        <p>
            BgQuiz is in beta, and the point of this round is to hear what breaks,
            what confuses, and what is missing.
            <a href="@AppInfo.FeedbackMailto">Email the BgQuiz beta address</a> — the
            subject line comes pre-filled with the exact build you are running
            (<code>@AppInfo.Version</code>), which is the first thing needed to make
            sense of a report.
        </p>

        @if (Controller.HasStarted && !Controller.IsFinished)
        {
            <button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" @onclick="BackToQuiz">
                Back to quiz
            </button>
        }

    </div>
</div>
